"Your vocal folds buzz at f₁ and make every harmonic, falling about {#} dB per octave once the sound leaves the lips (here aₙ = {#}/n, the sawtooth). The throat and mouth above them are a tube with resonances, the formants F1, F2 and F3, that pass the harmonics near them and weaken the rest. Moving the tongue and lips moves the formants, and that is the vowel: a low F1 and a high F2 make ee, a high F1 and a low F2 make ah, a low F1 and a low F2 make oo. Change the pitch and the harmonics slide under the same envelope: the vowel stays the same while the pitch changes. Formants are the averages for women in Peterson and Barney ({#}); the bandwidths {#}, {#} and {#} Hz are round values assumed for the model. With only {#} harmonics, a pitch below about {#} Hz no longer reaches F2 of ee.",
