"Detune one harmonic and the waveform stops repeating: its shape drifts through a full cycle every {#}/|Δf|, and you hear a slow, regular change at |Δf| per second. Mistuned by more than a few percent, the harmonic starts to stand out as a separate tone. Stiffness stretches every partial upward, fₙ = n f₁ √(({#} + Bn²)/({#} + B)), as in a stiff string; piano strings have a small B, one reason pianos are tuned with slightly wide octaves. A large B gives a clangy tone like a bell without one clear pitch. Real bells have their own particular partials; this only shows what inharmonic partials do.",
